torname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}x+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259714 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b12a7b7-1df3-5203-934e-8820604de3de" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30a15c72-9a15-5022-99b2-0c0968a3a7be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30a15c72-9a15-5022-99b2-0c0968a3a7be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259716} :answer-field/id #uuid "76bf53f9-dc9f-5679-8fdd-3f5f5d0ff6ed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259715} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "710df7e8-f09e-59ea-941f-dd92b0e48503" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "710df7e8-f09e-59ea-941f-dd92b0e48503" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259718} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b15d72a-1a4d-5b32-bf7a-3349b514d1cc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259717}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "889a0c6a-4567-5269-8c45-169860a4a405" :question/math-academy-id "q-332197" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{sin}(x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}(x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{cos}v-\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot 0-\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x \\\\  & =0\\,\\operatorname{sin}x+1\\,\\operatorname{cos}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260190 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7bc27633-cfde-532e-a38a-28d195fd2a5b" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Rational Expressions and Simplifying the Result by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ff748d4-caee-5df8-8fed-d7cc9b3c8f1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27x}{{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271924} {:answer/id #uuid "f479ad2b-1476-5ba0-afbb-9e53f76166e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{27x}{{y}^{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271925} {:answer/id #uuid "8a56b62f-b5a7-534a-829c-8bae48fcce9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\frac{27x}{{y}^{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271926} {:answer/id #uuid "c8622aa4-7013-58b5-b6c9-7d7a6ca0d05e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\frac{27x}{{y}^{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271927} {:answer/id #uuid "79df0b0d-eb1a-50d5-877c-5d197a53bf85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(\\frac{27x}{{y}^{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ff748d4-caee-5df8-8fed-d7cc9b3c8f1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27x}{{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271924} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4911542-11e2-514e-b1b3-0bb4d8a18972" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274664}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ea688c05-e239-5220-b902-15b6dea2eef8" :question/math-academy-id "q-3635" :question/problem "$\\frac{9x}{xy+5y}÷\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3x+15}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{9x}{xy+5y}÷\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3x+15},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9x}{xy+5y}÷\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3x+15} & = \\\\ \\frac{9x}{xy+5y}\\cdot \\frac{3x+15}{{y}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{9x}{y(x+5)}\\cdot \\frac{3(x+5)}{{y}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{9x}{y\\cancel{(x+5)}}\\cdot \\frac{3\\cancel{(x+5)}}{{y}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{9x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{3}{{y}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{27x}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260204 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4014376-4dae-56f1-b981-b35d3afab874" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Constant Such that the Limit of a Piecewise Function Exists"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90d7ac25-3873-59c3-b25f-4ce0e686e30e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "b = $(-1)-1$; c = $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271944} {:answer/id #uuid "92500148-04ef-5384-aa75-1d399017380e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "b = $(-1)-2$; c = $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271945} {:answer/id #uuid "7b435b66-be3e-5219-bfb4-33fdc82905e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "b = $(-1)+1$; c = $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271946} {:answer/id #uuid "bd74bb1c-f699-5657-8dd9-dbcd9e8883dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "b = $(-1)+2$; c = $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271947} {:answer/id #uuid "067904ef-5d42-559c-bf0c-885d75617f41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "b = $-1$; c = $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "067904ef-5d42-559c-bf0c-885d75617f41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "b = $-1$; c = $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271948} :answer-field/id #uuid "33a5c13f-378a-5d73-9805-cca4c35cb699" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274668}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3cbc081a-4656-5dca-b3b1-aa3f8536f992" :question/math-academy-id "q-36743" :question/problem "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x+b\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+cx+2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=2.$ What is the value of the constants $b$ and $c?$" :question/worked-solution "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ to be equal to $2,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $2.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}+2x+b.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}({x}^{2}+2x+b) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}+2(1)+b \\\\  & =1+2+b \\\\  & =b+3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left-sided limit must be equal to $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =2 \\\\ b+3 & =2 \\\\ b & =2-3 \\\\ b & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}+cx+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}({x}^{2}+cx+2) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}+c(1)+2 \\\\  & =1+c+2 \\\\  & =c+3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the right-sided limit must be equal to $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =2 \\\\ c+3 & =2 \\\\ c & =2-3 \\\\ c & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260229 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b04beff-987b-504f-9783-4465474e01ab" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Purely Imaginary Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8c76fff-e989-55d2-be4b-af84bb2ad69a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $(-3\\text{i})+1$; positive imaginary = $3\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271979} {:answer/id #uuid "73701cea-702e-527f-b163-5fbc39a0898b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $(-3\\text{i})-2$; positive imaginary = $3\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271980} {:answer/id #uuid "ec67003f-3f40-5aa8-b523-a3fca499c474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $(-3\\text{i})+2$; positive imaginary = $3\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271981} {:answer/id #uuid "3e66db6a-d814-5a1f-871e-c8061e35c40b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $-3\\text{i}$; positive imaginary = $3\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271982} {:answer/id #uuid "4683e9c8-aaa2-548c-9fb1-e075e963fdaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $(-3\\text{i})-1$; positive imaginary = $3\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e66db6a-d814-5a1f-871e-c8061e35c40b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $-3\\text{i}$; positive imaginary = $3\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271982} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8186316-3a16-548f-aded-8ad77f126e55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274675}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e7a2f2a-eb16-5a6f-9053-e49c72b4b60a" :question/math-academy-id "q-3783" :question/problem "$2{x}^{2}=-18$ has the solutions" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2} & =-18 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{18}{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking square root, we obtain:\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\sqrt{-9}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $x$ using the imaginary unit $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pm \\sqrt{-9} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{9\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260234 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b04beff-987b-504f-9783-4465474e01ab" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Purely Imaginary Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92361c3e-06f7-5a7a-bb12-a47627e42c92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $(-3\\text{i})+1$; positive imaginary = $3\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271984} {:answer/id #uuid "0ef780e2-d2b9-5332-8b0a-bbd0f6ae9ec1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $(-3\\text{i})-2$; positive imaginary = $3\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271985} {:answer/id #uuid "1a26fe64-dff8-51aa-8202-869e8804b473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $(-3\\text{i})+2$; positive imaginary = $3\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271986} {:answer/id #uuid "3592079c-2cc1-56d1-9594-64bdcd3d6ea1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $-3\\text{i}$; positive imaginary = $3\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271987} {:answer/id #uuid "62a698f7-845f-5564-8b03-fcd598f04d06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $(-3\\text{i})-1$; positive imaginary = $3\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3592079c-2cc1-56d1-9594-64bdcd3d6ea1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $-3\\text{i}$; positive imaginary = $3\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271987} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ff2620c-567e-58f8-87fb-0ddcbfc19d0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274676}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "77273b9c-44fa-5c95-bcfa-163635f82eb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-3808" :question/problem "Determine the solutions of the equation $3{r}^{2}+27=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{r}^{2}+27 & =0 \\\\ 3{r}^{2} & =-27 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking square root, we obtain:\n\n$$\nr=\\pm \\sqrt{-9}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $r$ using the imaginary unit $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\pm \\sqrt{-9} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{9\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99c4151a-d2e0-5603-b27d-d81c76a0c7ba" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of Reciprocal Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f39dc82-820e-5548-a0dd-c19e651b8a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260253} {:answer/id #uuid "203714a3-86e4-5086-a47e-056427b64a5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260254} {:answer/id #uuid "68357f1e-38a3-5f15-ada5-2918d0749d0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260255} {:answer/id #uuid "004fcd5a-d9cd-5ef1-9566-8524db4dcaa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260256} {:answer/id #uuid "fe083ecf-90fd-5715-aac8-b2ee888d250f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260257} {:answer/id #uuid "ee09beba-184e-52a2-8655-5ddd98441138" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260258} {:answer/id #uuid "32e51ac3-ebb0-5b54-8c8a-8c0e6a914bb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260259} {:answer/id #uuid "d09abb86-4cb1-5d8a-81b3-f062a0ae6192" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d09abb86-4cb1-5d8a-81b3-f062a0ae6192" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260260} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a811596-4561-58d6-aa4d-f28d49154dd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260252}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8c4a36da-599f-5664-ba9f-ac2ade1112ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-39273" :question/problem "For the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - The domain of the function is $x\\in (-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$\nII. - The graph of the function passes through the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2}).$\nIII. - $y\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ffd98b8f9b6c7a9260f43772f9c654901bd02c21efa1eb06f2a5fdad8b263729.png)\n\nNow, we analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is defined on all real numbers except $0.$ So, the domain of the function is $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. When $x=2,$ we get $y=\\frac{1}{2},$ so the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on the curve.\n\n- Statement III is false. From the graph, we see that $y\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f2d12ff-f760-5c03-88c0-2b7088eb5fb2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cubic Equation by Rearranging"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47373c27-c3ae-5d4c-8486-b42ea7c59a4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-2)+3,0,9\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272034} {:answer/id #uuid "2dec24fd-a3bd-59f8-917a-74a853f06493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-2)-1,0,9\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272035} {:answer/id #uuid "7fa63b5c-3b5c-52a5-8bfe-0fe6208e8e36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-2)-2,0,9\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272036} {:answer/id #uuid "63fdf231-60ce-5995-a5f1-c88d8fae51d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-2)+1,0,9\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272037} {:answer/id #uuid "850a7fb1-ce04-538e-b621-fc9730ea60df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-2,0,9\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "850a7fb1-ce04-538e-b621-fc9730ea60df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-2,0,9\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272038} :answer-field/id #uuid "64c174f0-e848-5957-86ee-8146678d9f7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274686}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "423dad75-ec6d-59c5-bce2-1028414952c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-3984" :question/problem "Solve for $m$ where $2{m}^{3}-5{m}^{2}-18m=2{m}^{2}+{m}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(m)=0.$ To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{m}^{3}-5{m}^{2}-18m & =2{m}^{2}+{m}^{3} \\\\ {m}^{3}-5{m}^{2}-18m & =2{m}^{2} \\\\ {m}^{3}-7{m}^{2}-18m & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $m.$ We can factor out the $m$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{3}-7{m}^{2}-18m & =0 \\\\ m({m}^{2}-7m-18) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression ${m}^{2}-7m-18$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m({m}^{2}-7m-18) & =0 \\\\ m(m+2)(m-9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $m=0,$ $m=-2,$ and $m=9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef75bc93-76da-5992-8285-181b808eb7a0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39b41caf-b96c-5db0-a05d-85ec4a3e0bb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,(2)+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272049} {:answer/id #uuid "42a8aed5-0248-591e-aa61-17ce2332af4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,(2)+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272050} {:answer/id #uuid "5103a52a-a6dc-5b13-93e9-b21d635364ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,(2)-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272051} {:answer/id #uuid "16831e20-a177-598a-9fb7-b19b2928f882" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272052} {:answer/id #uuid "830a1bc2-0c2c-5463-9225-10ee8699b3e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,(2)-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16831e20-a177-598a-9fb7-b19b2928f882" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272052} :answer-field/id #uuid "73a3b6e9-14d5-5890-aa40-39eb7afb3c51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274689}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99cb9382-c994-5be5-9048-a51a60032aa8" :question/math-academy-id "q-40384" :question/problem "What is the range of $y=-4(3)^{x}+2?$" :question/worked-solution "The range of ${3}^{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{3}^{x}>0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-4$ and then adding $2,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x} & >0 \\\\ -4(3)^{x} & <0 \\\\ -4(3)^{x}+2 & <0+2 \\\\ y & <2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,2).$ \n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $f(x)=-4(3)^{x}+2$ is shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/e3e926c26f8174dd5c92a5875e550530304fadcea4c964ab33b35e701f7e5ae5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260323 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8813b8a0-3b5e-5cd8-a37d-6b921f2882b8" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Repeating Decimal as the Sum of a Number and a Geometric Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a59094d-67ea-5ef2-9087-7241c30d6a4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left(2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{43}{10}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272064} {:answer/id #uuid "326cee98-7cb2-5587-9c1f-e8d5496664f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{43}{10}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272065} {:answer/id #uuid "c4691681-c4e6-5c91-b00a-ad6863ec8856" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left(2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{43}{10}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272066} {:answer/id #uuid "1dfa89a5-0cc9-55d8-a5bd-90d01729f54e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left(2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{43}{10}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272067} {:answer/id #uuid "1afd82e1-401f-5882-8bab-fdf4414e0479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12\\left(2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{43}{10}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "326cee98-7cb2-5587-9c1f-e8d5496664f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{43}{10}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272065} :answer-field/id #uuid "884028a8-36f1-5227-8efd-c82bb0672296" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274692}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "beb2fadf-9974-5f6b-99c2-f5a2d68db56e" :question/math-academy-id "q-40691" :question/problem "$2.3\\overset{―}{43}=$" :question/worked-solution "The given repeating decimal can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.3\\overset{―}{43} & =2.343\\,43... \\\\  & =2.3+0.043+0.000\\,43+0.000\\,004\\,3+… \\\\  & =2.3+{{\\frac{43}{1\\,000}+\\frac{43}{100\\,000}+\\frac{43}{10\\,000\\,000}+⋯.}_{⏟}}_{\\text{geometric series}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that we have a geometric series where the first term is ${a}_{1}=\\frac{43}{1\\,000}$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{1}{100}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.3\\overset{―}{43} & =2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{43}{1\\,000}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{43\\cdot 100}{1\\,000}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n} \\\\  & =2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{43}{10}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc8d4944-9005-5712-ad88-744dac83603e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Point of Intersection of a Circle and a Radial Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ee9499f-7c58-59f1-b29e-8e40f48219b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((7)+2,-7\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272097} {:answer/id #uuid "fce1f0be-3ba6-537b-a9dc-faacfa7f56e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,-7\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272098} {:answer/id #uuid "cc3c6067-1b7d-535b-b124-95c713c179e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((7)+1,-7\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272099} {:answer/id #uuid "5c491ed3-ef7d-5b07-9fa6-5c35a1797cfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((7)-1,-7\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272100} {:answer/id #uuid "c37603d3-1760-5f83-9aaa-3861a164afb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((7)-2,-7\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fce1f0be-3ba6-537b-a9dc-faacfa7f56e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,-7\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272098} :answer-field/id #uuid "7af15839-0458-5f55-a0c5-0b1d8bbeb66a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274699}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dce89418-8ba6-5cb9-88b6-4ca10b594bb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-41786" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/0836e8682e78ae6a53d9b5cf05bce78dd665765aca42504eb8774c6116f42520.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=14$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(14,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =14\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =14\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =7 \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\  & =14\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =14\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-7\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(7,-7\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260368 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ea83062-5744-52ef-9b3e-f9805ed092fb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to an Equation Containing a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14ff64a5-1a2c-591c-9dcd-f03785f89bff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272117} {:answer/id #uuid "f75b8c95-eec7-5d58-ad83-31a4bc7bb35e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272118} {:answer/id #uuid "e13592e3-6ca1-5b55-952d-7d4ccdbe2fd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "110" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272119} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea8fedb-7eea-5db4-9bc2-ecd493ef7b3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272120} {:answer/id #uuid "67ab8e73-977e-53fe-9109-5d677336e6c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14ff64a5-1a2c-591c-9dcd-f03785f89bff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272117} :answer-field/id #uuid "11e5b630-ab39-59bb-8e78-6e2d49052afd" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274703}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a9bd9256-8a79-56fe-a09d-da67096b964f" :question/math-academy-id "q-42787" :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric equation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=-\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+4\\pi n,\\,x=a+4\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n,\\,\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n, & \\,\\frac{x}{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n \\\\ \\frac{x}{2}=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}+2\\pi n, & \\,\\frac{x}{2}=\\frac{13\\pi }{12}+2\\pi n \\\\ x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+4\\pi n, & \\,x=\\frac{13\\pi }{6}+4\\pi n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $a=\\frac{13\\pi }{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdbfa506-08a1-5e43-b958-2ae373df775d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Linear Rational Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2960d52-8dcb-535a-a21d-790a645b8697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 2\\ln (K|5 − 3x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272415} {:answer/id #uuid "8530aff7-7f96-5e88-855e-eaa730d40120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (K|5 − 3x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272416} {:answer/id #uuid "9441359d-ff31-59db-83e1-6cfa565a16de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}(K|3x+4|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272417} {:answer/id #uuid "af4979c1-1fbf-5eb4-97fd-686cb1e41e4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\ln (K|5 − 3x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272418} {:answer/id #uuid "56929dde-4159-5a84-bdd7-cc2f060f6755" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2)\\ln (K|5 − 3x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9441359d-ff31-59db-83e1-6cfa565a16de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}(K|3x+4|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272417} :answer-field/id #uuid "d37d00b5-5824-52e8-b5d3-c9e7a78d4706" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274763}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c472c0d4-d3df-50b1-a45a-c2d9b5dcf85c" :question/math-academy-id "q-49515" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{5}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=3x+4.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{5}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}|3x+4|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}K$ for another arbitrary positive constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}|3x+4|+C & =\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}|3x+4|+\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}K \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}|3x+4|+\\operatorname{ln}K) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}(K|3x+4|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{5}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}(K|3x+4|).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4380b34-0502-552b-b94f-e121eb855f1d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Convergence Behavior of Geometric Sequences Written Recursively"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7493e4bf-895d-5e65-bb8f-bf2d383720b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260622} {:answer/id #uuid "209fa5c5-1466-5ce7-a8d2-8973f26a4e1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7493e4bf-895d-5e65-bb8f-bf2d383720b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260622} :answer-field/id #uuid "d20d3612-8f26-5ea3-b4eb-4ce596ec8c19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260621}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "71dac1d2-6ea4-558d-93e8-0311765a5a7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-49568" :question/problem "Consider the following sequence: \n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{5}{4}{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nDetermine whether the sequence is convergent or divergent. If it converges, find the limit." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nSince $|r|>1$, the sequence diverges."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260647 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d138d2c-3295-5eae-990d-42711f5792f1" :knowledge-point/title "Analyzing Statements Involving Sums of Convergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a35aa78-d547-5a55-8a2f-3f86e4c0afb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260649} {:answer/id #uuid "8644a362-1e72-5a40-acc5-710bc5a3bcd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260650} {:answer/id #uuid "e0dac5b6-786f-5744-93c0-87b1055f2912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260651} {:answer/id #uuid "38362d27-f96d-5548-bff5-1512c06ada80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260652} {:answer/id #uuid "0362b06e-efe4-545c-86bd-91cb1c3508c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260653} {:answer/id #uuid "acbfefc2-a7e5-5a3b-b04b-4a73976e50af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260654} {:answer/id #uuid "56f00260-986c-5669-a513-075f3979051c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260655} {:answer/id #uuid "e1267788-fb64-575f-b20c-448bc0e42758" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0dac5b6-786f-5744-93c0-87b1055f2912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260651} :answer-field/id #uuid "32524753-7bf7-51bd-b7ec-0c1ce05625a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260648}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f30cf6e1-a2f7-50a2-8c61-f5748c2a0e0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-49841" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - ${\\sum }_{n=100}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent\nIII. - $1\\,000{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the properties of convergent series:\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }K\\cdot {a}_{n}=K\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Removing a finite number of terms from a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\n- Statement II is false. The sum of two convergent series is convergent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Any constant multiple of a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fd76722-397f-564f-b592-52156e86ddc0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4743a3d-f288-5d0e-b3c3-f7d715298644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272500} {:answer/id #uuid "71c47003-56cc-5a68-a51b-147bca5ab6fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272501} {:answer/id #uuid "a6430f26-6f54-5255-8657-6f801d2f13c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272502} {:answer/id #uuid "cae0d96e-7c6f-57a8-b628-e8fe0ef1fdca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272503} {:answer/id #uuid "b5887fb2-946f-5c8c-b205-8df8a31fb4d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cae0d96e-7c6f-57a8-b628-e8fe0ef1fdca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272503} :answer-field/id #uuid "106b88ed-e7a8-5361-a89c-5a53b1754c20" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274780}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "51e5f7cc-f758-5b6e-9b53-61015feb7246" :question/math-academy-id "q-5174" :question/problem "If ${log}_{2}x=3$, then what is $x?$" :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $2,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}x & =3 \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}x} & ={2}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${2}^{{log}_{2}x}=x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={2}^{3} \\\\ x & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc45bf8f-2f0f-5ab9-b7d4-e9a3300b52a6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Purely Imaginary Fractional Roots With Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f90ad323-477f-55c3-b704-d6effe3cfad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\text{i})-2$; positive imaginary = $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272555} {:answer/id #uuid "db5a193e-cf1e-58be-b9c9-5cb14cfda799" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\text{i})+1$; positive imaginary = $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272556} {:answer/id #uuid "c8053b19-3656-5bda-bc9a-30b94daa31d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\text{i})+2$; positive imaginary = $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272557} {:answer/id #uuid "0102df07-72c4-5d32-99e5-199527ad3be4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\text{i})-1$; positive imaginary = $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272558} {:answer/id #uuid "cc63f23b-c858-546a-9e68-b408b5b4d6b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\text{i}$; positive imaginary = $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc63f23b-c858-546a-9e68-b408b5b4d6b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\text{i}$; positive imaginary = $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272559} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ca698d9-e6a8-595f-b3c0-febff72dc8fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274791}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "34a045e8-fdb7-5bf9-9392-e9e2795878f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-54632" :question/problem "What are the solutions to $-24{t}^{2}-2=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24{t}^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ -24{t}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =-\\frac{2}{24} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking square root, we obtain:\n\n$$\nt=\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{1}{12}}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $t$ using the imaginary unit $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{1}{12}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{12}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{1}}{\\sqrt{12}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{1}}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\text{i} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "831f9aa0-099e-5e0c-8a9d-c4828b629f64" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef898c1e-d9fd-5fc5-a3b2-eec2abe8e15f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272664} {:answer/id #uuid "747f9201-514b-5369-b32b-70d05b3dbe38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− \\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272665} {:answer/id #uuid "94c0dee0-cade-5849-8e5a-19c0769bab47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272666} {:answer/id #uuid "669f7bc5-f47d-5b7a-b784-944f3727762a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272667} {:answer/id #uuid "66f976c1-142e-5ac1-8488-8cd193d35356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− \\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94c0dee0-cade-5849-8e5a-19c0769bab47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272666} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d2bdce8-f6d8-51cd-b5d9-d083888cb53c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274816}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8bb03c0c-2bf3-5dda-8102-58698d5cc17e" :question/math-academy-id "q-65502" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{cos}v-\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "701015d7-cc6a-52ff-b40d-627a640cd0fb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Purely Imaginary Fractional Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "edb1b87e-0ff0-580f-8e0e-44b3ec2accd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $-\\frac{5}{2}\\text{i}$; positive imaginary = $\\frac{5}{2}\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272744} {:answer/id #uuid "5b1eadce-7650-52cf-9f0a-1290e623ad7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $(-\\frac{5}{2}\\text{i})+2$; positive imaginary = $\\frac{5}{2}\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272745} {:answer/id #uuid "362b5fe0-5f32-5cd3-8889-8bdb5117f46d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $(-\\frac{5}{2}\\text{i})+1$; positive imaginary = $\\frac{5}{2}\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272746} {:answer/id #uuid "af6645da-e212-553d-8953-89bb971704c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $(-\\frac{5}{2}\\text{i})-2$; positive imaginary = $\\frac{5}{2}\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272747} {:answer/id #uuid "7a905bec-21e7-5b54-aa6f-e3051c960284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $(-\\frac{5}{2}\\text{i})-1$; positive imaginary = $\\frac{5}{2}\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edb1b87e-0ff0-580f-8e0e-44b3ec2accd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $-\\frac{5}{2}\\text{i}$; positive imaginary = $\\frac{5}{2}\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272744} :answer-field/id #uuid "210fe317-9ffb-579a-a44e-d39edab77835" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274832}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f724ceca-0067-5fb3-8235-2b58d83a773f" :question/math-academy-id "q-66252" :question/problem "If $4{y}^{2}+25=0,$ then $y=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ 4{y}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking square root, we obtain:\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{25}{4}}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $y$ using the imaginary unit $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{25}}{\\sqrt{4}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d076e5d-9d08-5355-b813-d1749feb0803" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions to Exponential Equations when Rearrangement is Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ead4bb78-d334-5c81-9452-c9e922814601" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.916" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272823} {:answer/id #uuid "2da13e15-4a49-5fbc-b901-718da2cdcd5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.584" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272824} {:answer/id #uuid "a81e6f11-7ac2-5e8c-981c-3d34205f52e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.896" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272825} {:answer/id #uuid "c2da04cb-d0d0-55b7-94ba-aa45e02d784b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{4}(11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272826} {:answer/id #uuid "eb33c2c1-9828-5781-85f5-9870845f11ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.792" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2da04cb-d0d0-55b7-94ba-aa45e02d784b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{4}(11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272826} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed529180-b979-5a7d-b4b8-b435d7f0da7a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274848}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "201b2cc7-e621-5a40-94ff-980ef3cc44e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-66832" :question/problem "Find the exact solution to the equation $3({4}^{y})-14=19.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({4}^{y})-14 & =19 \\\\ 3({4}^{y}) & =33 \\\\ {4}^{y} & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take ${log}_{4}$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}({4}^{y}) & ={log}_{4}(11) \\\\ y & ={log}_{4}(11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y={log}_{4}(11).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261128 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b024b05-1efe-594e-9a7e-6d51a28c0c78" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations by First Rearranging the Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dc02679-4027-5a89-b36c-ce998775fdf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273023} {:answer/id #uuid "bf3e4511-f0e7-5105-98a9-1c55f96ee62f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273024} {:answer/id #uuid "55d6ed52-178c-5a33-b99b-fea5ea9ff28d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273025} {:answer/id #uuid "6e192cbd-96e3-5b52-a340-e2a724c3afb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273026} {:answer/id #uuid "61ebd3ce-5f2b-5f4a-9021-ccb177602e33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf3e4511-f0e7-5105-98a9-1c55f96ee62f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273024} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a06a18b-fd30-566e-9e60-d5d63fbfc657" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274888}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "513d3cec-982b-533e-81bb-8c54a8125de4" :question/math-academy-id "q-70017" :question/problem "If ${log}_{3}(x-6)+5=8$, then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}(x-6)+5 & =8 \\\\ {log}_{3}(x-6) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $3,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}(x-6) & =3 \\\\ {3}^{{log}_{3}(x-6)} & ={3}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${3}^{{log}_{3}(x-6)}=x-6.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-6 & ={3}^{3} \\\\ x-6 & =27 \\\\ x & =33.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b024b05-1efe-594e-9a7e-6d51a28c0c78" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations by First Rearranging the Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c18b406-0990-5146-9638-a1af0647fd14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273028} {:answer/id #uuid "14f844a6-6193-5c2f-b8d1-2bdf501683cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273029} {:answer/id #uuid "12aa322c-b7a8-54de-b80f-35ea00c2b284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273030} {:answer/id #uuid "3e7b5b2c-6409-516c-96cc-f31262e21285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273031} {:answer/id #uuid "6d4dbf82-6a71-5b5c-9458-4062c1b1d847" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e7b5b2c-6409-516c-96cc-f31262e21285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273031} :answer-field/id #uuid "04478482-8d9f-50f1-b66f-43bc8cb68e96" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274889}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6670ae0c-7bd3-5be8-8afd-7e9eadd30b6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-70018" :question/problem "If $6{log}_{4}(x+11)=12$, then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{log}_{4}(x+11) & =12 \\\\ {log}_{4}(x+11) & =\\frac{12}{6} \\\\ {log}_{4}(x+11) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $4,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}(x+11) & =2 \\\\ {4}^{{log}_{4}(x+11)} & ={4}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${4}^{{log}_{4}(x+11)}=x+11.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+11 & ={4}^{2} \\\\ x+11 & =16 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "993a1649-c534-5a08-805f-3388c2f83b20" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Rational Expressions with No Common Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c299822-bf2f-58dd-971e-82496b96c4e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5ab)/(14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273078} {:answer/id #uuid "4caab637-e633-565a-b1cf-ebff3f1e210c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16a}{21b}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273079} {:answer/id #uuid "a92858ce-dd72-55f9-9782-a1f6e4878f0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5a)/(14b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273080} {:answer/id #uuid "f4b226e3-6914-5dba-bb3a-c1ffa7586aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5ab)/(7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273081} {:answer/id #uuid "929ca7a1-6226-54b1-8981-3fa9facb2562" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5b)/(14a)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4caab637-e633-565a-b1cf-ebff3f1e210c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16a}{21b}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273079} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d1af6f8-95d7-513e-b767-242c3da1631d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274899}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4d031f53-2cb0-5ef4-9eb5-4fffa3c8a6c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-70350" :question/problem "What is the result of dividing $\\frac{8a}{3}$ by $\\frac{7b}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{8a}{3}÷\\frac{7b}{2},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8a}{3}÷\\frac{7b}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{8a}{3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{7b} & = \\\\ \\frac{16a}{21b} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f149f8ef-dd7f-5f65-928e-534e1541f9b2" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Rational Expressions with a Single Common Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59c3eb4f-3413-5a1f-8c7c-55650a86f25b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(35)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273083} {:answer/id #uuid "f37a2d8d-d22c-5570-8bde-f78eb0725024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273084} {:answer/id #uuid "fbf0f645-fbcd-5a92-a589-cf5bec78938d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273085} {:answer/id #uuid "11b2207f-9b7e-558e-a40d-7c0793e85a2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273086} {:answer/id #uuid "e35af1b5-4ef5-5979-863d-ed143909fccd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(15)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbf0f645-fbcd-5a92-a589-cf5bec78938d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273085} :answer-field/id #uuid "a527be40-4892-5a14-8fc8-2905068f0e36" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274900}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "deb08641-7369-57ac-a4cc-afb3b4f81d58" :question/math-academy-id "q-70355" :question/problem "$\\frac{14}{a}÷\\frac{7}{b}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{14}{a}÷\\frac{7}{b},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{14}{a}÷\\frac{7}{b} & = \\\\ \\frac{14}{a}\\cdot \\frac{b}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{14b}{7a} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{7}\\cdot b}{\\cancel{7}a} & = \\\\ \\frac{2b}{a} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the final step, we canceled out a common factor of $7$ in the numerator and denominator."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cef7fc21-23f4-5d6a-a208-9963ef23c784" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent Line of a Logarithmic Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "134a29b9-8f28-564f-a103-b402b8d39f78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}2}+4\\operatorname{ln}2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273168} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc6a654-bd43-5067-824c-5692a4922eef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln 3 + 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273169} {:answer/id #uuid "4b1ec3b9-e4e2-5880-a5ee-cd5a4c65f98a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(\\ln 3) + 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273170} {:answer/id #uuid "2b364ac9-f3bd-5352-a258-e1617ab227e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(\\ln 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273171} {:answer/id #uuid "e1868c62-3903-51df-a544-728227a3e34e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5)/(\\ln 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "134a29b9-8f28-564f-a103-b402b8d39f78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}2}+4\\operatorname{ln}2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273168} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cf464a9-3c68-599c-8f43-c2a6974908d8" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274917}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9194b183-89a8-5cf0-9567-8d024e74d060" :question/math-academy-id "q-70897" :question/problem "Given that $y=2{log}_{2}x+{2}^{x}$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}2}+{2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}2\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & ={(\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}2}+{2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}2)|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}2}+{2}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}2}+4\\operatorname{ln}2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261348 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e25f87c9-3f2e-59a6-ad45-66f0640e68ef" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cubic Equation With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe31da27-84e6-52a5-bcdf-edef11e7170a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(0)-2,2,5\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273273} {:answer/id #uuid "30fd5390-94e1-5f16-aa5b-08c638d84f4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(0)+1,2,5\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273274} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f7854a-a070-59fc-bc17-2bd1acfd76b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{0,2,5\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273275} {:answer/id #uuid "dce898b3-f1f2-5dd1-83aa-4f34a9d594f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(0)-1,2,5\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273276} {:answer/id #uuid "abb4b502-a8be-54e1-815e-7f6810531708" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(0)+3,2,5\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8f7854a-a070-59fc-bc17-2bd1acfd76b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{0,2,5\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273275} :answer-field/id #uuid "997cce29-2e8e-57b9-8b8e-b33e6959fcfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274938}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56a901dc-9638-58ff-af9a-f87cff2b0a14" :question/math-academy-id "q-71778" :question/problem "Solve the equation $5{z}^{3}-35{z}^{2}+50z=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $5z.$ We can factor out the $5z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{z}^{3}-35{z}^{2}+50z & =0 \\\\ 5z({z}^{2}-7z+10) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression ${z}^{2}-7z+10$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5z({z}^{2}-7z+10) & =0 \\\\ 5z(z-2)(z-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $z=0,$ $z=2,$ and $z=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261355 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3974b5c6-129f-5616-be84-08b6ab779f70" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Higher-Order Polynomial Equation With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d88a6dac-da22-570a-8824-0dac01a8ea23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\frac13)+1,0,\\frac72\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273278} {:answer/id #uuid "901cdee8-8c8f-5caa-ba97-1f2063007fe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\frac13)-1,0,\\frac72\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273279} {:answer/id #uuid "58b0e70e-3831-5a25-b937-f574216dc641" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\frac13)-2,0,\\frac72\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273280} {:answer/id #uuid "2568afad-97c2-50c0-a380-ecdb120090b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-\\frac13,0,\\frac72\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273281} {:answer/id #uuid "a28d7b9c-664d-5211-9180-a0cd2639daf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\frac13)+2,0,\\frac72\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2568afad-97c2-50c0-a380-ecdb120090b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-\\frac13,0,\\frac72\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273281} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5161011-7e82-5c6b-9818-4c34f8969b7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274939}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bfbcf977-a1e8-5bf7-8080-66924ccaab82" :question/math-academy-id "q-71782" :question/problem "Find the roots of the polynomial $p(t)=30{t}^{5}-95{t}^{4}-35{t}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the roots of the polynomial, we must solve the equation $p(t)=0,$ that is\n\n$$\n30{t}^{5}-95{t}^{4}-35{t}^{3}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $5{t}^{3}.$ We can factor out the $5{t}^{3}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{t}^{3}(6{t}^{2}-19t-7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $6{t}^{2}-19t-7$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{t}^{3}(6{t}^{2}-19t-7) & =0 \\\\ 5{t}^{3}(3t+1)(2t-7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $t=0,$ $t=-\\frac{1}{3},$ and $t=\\frac{7}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecb9df79-5227-5fc6-9a20-818fee34e971" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "243bae6a-7f90-509c-a611-d0cd92e56106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273288} {:answer/id #uuid "5f1350aa-51a5-5918-9989-a72de659b6ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(55)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273289} {:answer/id #uuid "e3685375-90e3-5409-b4a8-619b07aa258e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(55)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273290} {:answer/id #uuid "bd9a05e4-f6ec-5ed5-82f3-6f76662411d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(55)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273291} {:answer/id #uuid "5d29a48d-bc3f-54a0-a202-80512e7411b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(55)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "243bae6a-7f90-509c-a611-d0cd92e56106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273288} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc98e4b3-0f9d-51fe-abc9-ac2aaa742645" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274941}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "71771640-36aa-5365-9496-11f66f198e7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-72064" :question/problem "The following table shows the number of customers per minute, $C(t),$ that arrive at a bank, measured over a period of $15$ minutes.\n\n| $t\\,\\text{(minutes)}$ | $0$ | $3$ | $7$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $C(t)\\,\\text{(customers/minute)}$ | $3$ | $4$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum over $[0,15]$ with four subintervals to approximate the total number of customers served." :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ denote the area under the curve $y=C(t)$ over $[0,15].$ In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is approximated as \n\n$$\nA\\approx C(3)Δ{t}_{1}+C(7)Δ{t}_{2}+C(12)Δ{t}_{3}+C(15)Δ{t}_{4},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{t}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $t$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{t}_{i}={t}_{i}-{t}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δt$'s.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $3$ | $7$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δt$ | $-$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n| $C(t)$ | $-$ | $4$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nWe plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & \\approx 4\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 4+5\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12+12+25+6 \\\\  & =55.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total number of customers served over a period of $15$ minutes. So, the total number of customers is approximately\n$55.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "309c730b-1c01-53a3-8883-b8a542ef1f17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin x\\csc^{2} (\\cos x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273302} {:answer/id #uuid "6cd2ff13-7da6-5dbb-bc00-02759922efd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin x\\cot^{2} (\\cos x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273303} {:answer/id #uuid "1b7e69a5-231b-5eb3-b270-6a8b4631fd45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin x\\cos^{2} (\\csc x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273304} {:answer/id #uuid "110b4e0a-e914-5250-81cf-93136df46969" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\csc x\\sin^{2} (\\cos x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273305} {:answer/id #uuid "198f33a0-3165-508a-9334-b8b8086d828e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\operatorname{sec}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "198f33a0-3165-508a-9334-b8b8086d828e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\operatorname{sec}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273306} :answer-field/id #uuid "0062b138-c3b9-5cc5-bc14-5885d95d6202" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274944}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f5156b8-2062-5adc-85c3-14a0aa909be9" :question/math-academy-id "q-72163" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=\\operatorname{sin}(\\operatorname{sec}x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}u,\\,u=\\operatorname{sec}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{cos}u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}u\\cdot (\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\operatorname{sec}x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41ff6c60-0496-59ed-8942-e2c8279a45e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "time s = $2$; velocity m per s = $-1/e^2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273407} {:answer/id #uuid "ac445ed8-952c-5de1-a6c0-5dc70b0c8824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "time s = $(2)-2$; velocity m per s = $-1/e^2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273408} {:answer/id #uuid "0804e507-30be-5121-94cc-9076f9654b62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "time s = $(2)+1$; velocity m per s = $-1/e^2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273409} {:answer/id #uuid "8eaa79a7-0886-5ca5-aa46-640b99b4fc38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "time s = $(2)+2$; velocity m per s = $-1/e^2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273410} {:answer/id #uuid "200b4d45-b33d-5820-8836-b437c8a6186d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "time s = $(2)-1$; velocity m per s = $-1/e^2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41ff6c60-0496-59ed-8942-e2c8279a45e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "time s = $2$; velocity m per s = $-1/e^2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273407} :answer-field/id #uuid "f80de19d-031f-542d-9602-c6d7f9e5629d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274965}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9016b9c4-555f-5f54-8e92-6ab14881289d" :question/math-academy-id "q-73243" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=t{e}^{-t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(1-t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((1-t){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(t-2){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(t-2){e}^{-t} \\\\ t-2 & =0 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=2.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =(1-(2)){e}^{-(2)} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11263ba1-2ef9-5c93-8c56-d09dcbea8f8b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum of a Geometric Series Given Two Consecutive Terms of the Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e54e5c89-c83b-5423-b43e-83d1925f845c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "433" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273422} {:answer/id #uuid "a90dcdb2-36c5-5b94-84b7-dfefb2a13566" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "519" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273423} {:answer/id #uuid "9095c23b-ff1e-5941-869d-8cabd48a59bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "326" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273424} {:answer/id #uuid "4fbaea85-2c65-5fc4-930b-de355262d977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "127" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273425} {:answer/id #uuid "be06a76b-75fb-5b52-9651-8d15c24e78e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "292" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fbaea85-2c65-5fc4-930b-de355262d977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "127" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273425} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5c6546d-012a-58a9-9649-b5c3fb0835c3" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274968}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a1680abc-bbec-518f-a962-a951e4463528" :question/math-academy-id "q-73306" :question/problem "The fifth term of a geometric series is $4$ and the sixth term is $2.$ What is the sum of the first $7$ terms?" :question/worked-solution "The sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term of the series, and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nTo find $r,$ we find the ratio between the consecutive terms ${a}_{5}$ and ${a}_{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{6}}{{a}_{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we must determine the value of ${a}_{1}.$ To do this, we use the formula for the $n$th term, which states that\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=5,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5-1} \\\\ 4 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4} \\\\ 4 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{16} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{4}{(\\frac{1}{16})} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, if we substitute ${a}_{1}=64,N=7$ and $r=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the formula for ${S}_{N},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{7} & =64\\cdot \\frac{1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{7}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =64\\cdot \\frac{1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{7}}{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{(\\frac{1}{2})}\\cdot (1-\\frac{1}{128}) \\\\  & =128(1-\\frac{1}{128}) \\\\  & =128-1 \\\\  & =127\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00d82333-e4e0-5527-a084-0af8affe5bee" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6876d967-13f8-5ac6-aa24-eac1c9701487" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273476} {:answer/id #uuid "2c8c42d7-89f3-5e87-9191-8b057539f2e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3110" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273477} {:answer/id #uuid "cd9bb0e9-442b-5e14-8e5d-a80e29e93455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273478} {:answer/id #uuid "9bbeb22e-c5b5-5eed-9760-0ab182a73efd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1070" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273479} {:answer/id #uuid "6c132005-423a-5b49-b552-b69bcd914e9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2140" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6876d967-13f8-5ac6-aa24-eac1c9701487" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273476} :answer-field/id #uuid "29eed7a1-3c5f-594c-8775-e226be9c8e29" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274979}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41763944-ffc1-5cbe-ae34-8d6954078ad7" :question/math-academy-id "q-74505" :question/problem "The following table shows some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[2,12].$\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nUse the left Riemann sum, with the four subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[2,12].$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the left Riemann sum, the area is approximated as\n\n$$\nA\\approx f(2)Δ{x}_{1}+f(4)Δ{x}_{2}+f(8)Δ{x}_{3}+f(10)Δ{x}_{4},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the next one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i+1}-{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $2$ | $4$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & \\approx 5\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =10+12+4+2 \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e520dc67-3b63-5ca7-959b-f642d85529d6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Magnitude of a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6820a833-90da-5078-bd99-a6f3533f34c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273511} {:answer/id #uuid "8adcd3a7-6fd0-5592-88b6-18748468da00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273512} {:answer/id #uuid "96b9e322-b08b-53ea-8bf2-57aa14505eb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273513} {:answer/id #uuid "44f835f4-261b-5f7c-bc7f-6c0931932945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273514} {:answer/id #uuid "7a70a956-4d43-56ff-86e6-1c416ddf48d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96b9e322-b08b-53ea-8bf2-57aa14505eb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273513} :answer-field/id #uuid "d18528b8-58a0-5dc8-bcd7-edcab5c58076" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274986}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a178c1c-f11d-5828-ae41-32c929e1c753" :question/math-academy-id "q-75001" :question/problem "Find $|r(\\frac{\\pi }{3})|$ given that $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}t,\\,6⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating the function at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,4\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2},\\,4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2\\sqrt{3},\\,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the magnitude of this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r(\\frac{\\pi }{3})| & =\\sqrt{{(1)}^{2}+{(2\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+(6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+12+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261584 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0adffc7-ef35-5f7a-a790-06addb354c2e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum to Infinity of a Geometric Series Given Two of Its Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91cc4455-09b7-5290-9e25-747afac4f350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273526} {:answer/id #uuid "d71ac27d-229e-5293-9466-a762aac8ed91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273527} {:answer/id #uuid "e0955fc0-ff48-542d-8f19-65f9991b61bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273528} {:answer/id #uuid "c964fec9-19ee-590c-bce3-c2e4d3ebb8c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273529} {:answer/id #uuid "538a7990-38e7-59da-9b91-126b2f846866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91cc4455-09b7-5290-9e25-747afac4f350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273526} :answer-field/id #uuid "607c4bd5-70b9-52bd-b7a5-3b2291b1929f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274989}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64aca08e-6aad-5814-b106-f9f74dd6f182" :question/math-academy-id "q-75637" :question/problem "Consider the positive infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $56$ and a third term equal to $\\frac{7}{8}.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{1}=56$ and ${a}_{3}=\\frac{7}{8}.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{3-1} & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{8})}{56} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =(\\frac{7}{8})(\\frac{1}{56}) \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{64} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{64}} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we have taken the positive square root since we're told that the sequence is positive.\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{(1-\\frac{1}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{(\\frac{7}{8})} \\\\  & =56(\\frac{8}{7}) \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c698912-1f35-5110-8d5d-d490cce2e9a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Quadratic Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6e52b7c-0f41-5e6a-b88c-323acbfef6e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273551} {:answer/id #uuid "7c987a70-a554-57f2-a7a5-596ecc2715bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273552} {:answer/id #uuid "708a7f93-7c0a-5350-b4aa-d99797a71d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273553} {:answer/id #uuid "9157de8b-eaeb-5151-842c-b5d458782cac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273554} {:answer/id #uuid "d0637c39-5487-5972-9e2e-acc5a04d2948" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6e52b7c-0f41-5e6a-b88c-323acbfef6e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273551} :answer-field/id #uuid "5acb0b7e-4183-537a-a773-7b0e0cce9857" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274994}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7212979-12af-55e0-b574-fe7390124c7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-75961" :question/problem "Which value of $y$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{y+1}{{y}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{2y}+\\frac{2y-1}{{y}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $y=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators ${y}^{2}$ and $2y.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}({y}^{2},2y)=2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2{y}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+1}{{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{3}{2y}+\\frac{2y-1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2{y}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{y+1}{{y}^{2}}) & =2{y}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2y}+\\frac{2y-1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\ 2{y}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{y+1}{{y}^{2}}) & =2{y}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2y})+2{y}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2y-1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\ 2\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{y+1}{{y}^{2}}) & =y\\cdot 2y\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2y})+2\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2y-1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\ 2\\cdot \\cancel{{y}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{y+1}{\\cancel{{y}^{2}}}) & =y\\cdot \\cancel{2y}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{\\cancel{2y}})+2\\cdot \\cancel{{y}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{2y-1}{\\cancel{{y}^{2}}}) \\\\ 2(y+1) & =3y+2(2y-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $y,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(y+1) & =3y+2(2y-1) \\\\ 2y+2 & =7y-2 \\\\ 7y-2y & =2+2 \\\\ 5y & =4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $y=0$ as a solution. But since $y=\\frac{4}{5}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $y=\\frac{4}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "386bce9a-0c89-545c-b9ed-b9f67fb836b2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Purely Imaginary Solutions With Simplification"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ffa82b4-6837-5d46-a546-655834c8eb28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $(-5\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})+1$; positive imaginary = $5\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273576} {:answer/id #uuid "99bb7cce-4170-5d84-a340-d6194dbba021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $(-5\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})-1$; positive imaginary = $5\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273577} {:answer/id #uuid "2054777a-c54e-5053-93b9-d270ce8e2569" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $(-5\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})+2$; positive imaginary = $5\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273578} {:answer/id #uuid "25d1e8c6-070b-5cc4-a100-98d40269024b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $(-5\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})-2$; positive imaginary = $5\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273579} {:answer/id #uuid "2d4f2652-e38a-5abb-a3b9-3c4e5e520db8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $-5\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}$; positive imaginary = $5\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d4f2652-e38a-5abb-a3b9-3c4e5e520db8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $-5\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}$; positive imaginary = $5\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273580} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6cc0b06-66fe-5809-a8f7-bf0eca8395a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274999}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e678503-4a31-59f2-91e3-8d0ec7418e01" :question/math-academy-id "q-76538" :question/problem "If $\\frac{{p}^{2}}{5}+15=0,$ then $p=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{p}^{2}}{5}+15 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{p}^{2}}{5} & =-15 \\\\ {p}^{2} & =-75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking square root, we obtain:\n\n$$\np=\\pm \\sqrt{-75}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $p$ using the imaginary unit $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =\\pm \\sqrt{-75} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{75\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{75}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261619 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "701015d7-cc6a-52ff-b40d-627a640cd0fb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Purely Imaginary Fractional Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec210cd6-c239-5dc9-b33e-e15dcedafe96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $(-\\frac{1}{3}\\text{i})+1$; positive imaginary = $\\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273581} {:answer/id #uuid "0cd6e607-97d6-5d38-b0bc-c92184fa9c80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $(-\\frac{1}{3}\\text{i})-1$; positive imaginary = $\\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273582} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ec7111-7b51-50b6-9265-ed460efb0f2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $(-\\frac{1}{3}\\text{i})-2$; positive imaginary = $\\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273583} {:answer/id #uuid "cbda7746-e522-5412-93d5-054ba64795d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $-\\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}$; positive imaginary = $\\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273584} {:answer/id #uuid "99f1b8c7-9802-5e83-b1f0-18655b72e8c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $(-\\frac{1}{3}\\text{i})+2$; positive imaginary = $\\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbda7746-e522-5412-93d5-054ba64795d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative imaginary = $-\\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}$; positive imaginary = $\\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273584} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c3cc3dc-9fda-5f24-bbd6-82f3fdb4cacc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275000}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a80650b-addc-581e-a84e-299da6eb427d" :question/math-academy-id "q-76539" :question/problem "If $9{z}^{2}+1=0$, then $z=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{z}^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ 9{z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking square root, we obtain:\n\n$$\nz=\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{1}{9}}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $z$ using the imaginary unit $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{1}}{\\sqrt{9}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261733 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98fb5d45-5215-506c-8b2a-99aaadb6631c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations With Linear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67162d78-c1da-59da-9d9c-63d4d703731d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273691} {:answer/id #uuid "a54d6b00-e4ed-5125-93b5-01dc002fa0c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273692} {:answer/id #uuid "0446d7e1-080d-5c54-934d-83ba4cb5baab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273693} {:answer/id #uuid "9968974e-ed31-5628-a6a8-65dda75f7b4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273694} {:answer/id #uuid "e39efc4c-2680-5aba-b004-901cfc2d864e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9968974e-ed31-5628-a6a8-65dda75f7b4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273694} :answer-field/id #uuid "4513b593-316c-5fb3-8064-63367f896134" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275022}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "036e1450-c076-5d46-8a8b-161ca824e7b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79380" :question/problem "If ${log}_{9}(2y-3)=1$, then $y=$" :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $9,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{9}(2y-3) & =1 \\\\ {9}^{{log}_{9}(2y-3)} & ={9}^{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${9}^{{log}_{9}(2y-3)}=2y-3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-3 & =9 \\\\ 2y & =12 \\\\ y & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "310db608-6d8b-51af-8abd-60f4dbd05423" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations by Combining Logarithmic Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a091d40a-bd8e-5895-9153-9e8e64f421d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273696} {:answer/id #uuid "38a6d5d3-2236-5b48-ae57-c4935249a5ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273697} {:answer/id #uuid "6d21a8d0-175b-56c9-b63d-4ebf6df02930" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273698} {:answer/id #uuid "55e3e0c4-e4e5-59de-a7b6-de9ab92ea2fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273699} {:answer/id #uuid "27e10c8f-393d-501a-8a87-8bd1a3f25642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d21a8d0-175b-56c9-b63d-4ebf6df02930" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273698} :answer-field/id #uuid "c66fac13-e197-517e-ae33-fc38336f8233" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275023}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "085c7d1a-1e1a-5955-8d31-2be9a5ca85e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-79389" :question/problem "If $5{log}_{4}x+3{log}_{4}x=16$, then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, and isolating the logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{log}_{4}x+3{log}_{4}x & =16 \\\\ (5+3){log}_{4}x & =16 \\\\ 8{log}_{4}x & =16 \\\\ {log}_{4}x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $4,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}x & =2 \\\\ {4}^{{log}_{4}x} & ={4}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${4}^{{log}_{4}x}=x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={4}^{2} \\\\ x & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90b485f8-a07d-55f4-8cde-f699070eeae7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Monotonic Polynomial and Rational Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "baac2114-91db-50b1-a268-6631f4f42b1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261771} {:answer/id #uuid "079f0eeb-7453-5945-9b06-9e1dad690e83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261772} {:answer/id #uuid "8359f964-b89c-5b20-ba35-f8bbc3c43e56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261773} {:answer/id #uuid "8f18f449-3068-57cf-a6d1-faeaf52320da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261774} {:answer/id #uuid "8d243a0b-6222-5ef8-b72d-15bf1f4e8539" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261775} {:answer/id #uuid "13337942-1f12-53a6-aac8-e02e3958ef3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261776} {:answer/id #uuid "f0869464-02da-5c0c-8d4c-9fce35de8702" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261777} {:answer/id #uuid "73973f3e-ceb9-5f33-833e-e687f2dcc6e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8359f964-b89c-5b20-ba35-f8bbc3c43e56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261773} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfdad0fd-561d-5abc-9287-2caf6255bd83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261770}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "752a7942-16ed-5bb1-8e0f-a8b976101dd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-81074" :question/problem "Given that  ${a}_{n}=\\frac{5}{(n+1)^{2}}$ for $n\\ge 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1$\nII. - ${a}_{n}$ is an increasing sequence\nIII. - ${a}_{n}$ is a decreasing sequence" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is a positive sequence defined for $n\\ge 1,$ and that\n\n$$\nL=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *increasing,* and\n\n- if $0<L\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case, ${a}_{n}=\\frac{5}{(n+1)^{2}}$ is positive for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the ratio $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{5}{(n+2)^{2}})}{(\\frac{5}{(n+1)^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(n+1)^{2}}{5(n+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n+1)^{2}}{(n+2)^{2}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{n+1}{n+2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $n\\ge 1,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\frac{n+1}{n+2}<1,\n$$\n\nsince the numerator is always smaller than the denominator. Therefore,\n\n$$\n0<L<1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, whereas statement III is true. Since $0<L\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ we can conclude that the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8aaf12cf-8596-5960-bc68-5ca3eb5fab0f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c262389f-a9f2-5146-b4e6-f80491ede8c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273918} {:answer/id #uuid "4e164b3a-7510-5b1d-9827-7de708d64131" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (1)/(25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273919} {:answer/id #uuid "2bfab4f6-3b74-5d3a-bdba-f3eb0b955b90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273920} {:answer/id #uuid "3dcbd4f8-f4c4-5328-ba59-b5440b930ca5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273921} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4622e5-b893-510a-9bc1-3d8fc7a6150c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4)/(25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c262389f-a9f2-5146-b4e6-f80491ede8c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273918} :answer-field/id #uuid "bff950a4-4c44-5c09-a1a8-6613c190447f" :answer-field/key "product-of-solutions" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275068}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8148e6a-df0b-54f7-92fc-57c9f0d3a4be" :question/math-academy-id "q-88472" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $4{x}^{2}{e}^{5x}=3{e}^{5x}-4x{e}^{5x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{e}^{5x} & =3{e}^{5x}-4x{e}^{5x} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}{e}^{5x}+4x{e}^{5x}-3{e}^{5x} & =0 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}+4x-3){e}^{5x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+4x-3=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{e}^{5x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $4{x}^{2}+4x-3=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2x+3)(2x-1)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The equation ${e}^{5x}=0$ has no solutions because ${e}^{5x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2},$ and their product is $(-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{3}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fd76722-397f-564f-b592-52156e86ddc0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11e63763-0230-54c0-8bb8-5a774b71e7d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273938} {:answer/id #uuid "26517c0c-1be9-5351-9399-50f89f3633ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273939} {:answer/id #uuid "6b3e26b6-d8ea-5d7a-a445-d085acfb07f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273940} {:answer/id #uuid "3c73e69e-5904-5f04-bbbf-935abf7ac982" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273941} {:answer/id #uuid "437f3021-4339-5ede-a35a-683395d34c15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "437f3021-4339-5ede-a35a-683395d34c15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273942} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d81b05b-8e2d-585f-acbd-099a88dad524" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275072}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f443508-b423-5aae-b226-2edd43a6873e" :question/math-academy-id "q-914" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{log}y=3$, then $y=$" :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the common logarithm is $10,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}y & =3 \\\\ {log}_{10}y & =3 \\\\ {10}^{{log}_{10}y} & ={10}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${10}^{{log}_{10}y}=y.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={10}^{3} \\\\ y & =1\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c2518d5-0fde-5dfc-aa07-f1bdd3c1585a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the End-Behavior of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab8ca3c6-1dc2-5092-ad69-7acecde4344f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273963} {:answer/id #uuid "28ce21d2-2bd5-5dbf-8443-b7cc3e3ccbac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273964} {:answer/id #uuid "2ab795ff-90da-550c-bd21-e359870eb512" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273965} {:answer/id #uuid "e56ba3c4-46bf-5f19-91ae-22071f83aa21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273966} {:answer/id #uuid "85f65b32-4726-5fe8-b6f1-a89b36337560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28ce21d2-2bd5-5dbf-8443-b7cc3e3ccbac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273964} :answer-field/id #uuid "7042d5e2-c041-5d1f-9845-6b855f9db331" :answer-field/key "y" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275077}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f44d5537-7cca-5193-b04b-aab03731cb25" :question/math-academy-id "q-92985" :question/problem "Find the equation of the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x-3}+4.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/47d570c5b05096a0e8b7a13df510d8cd71dbee93dd2bf58dae18d7ff49ff1787.png)\n\nSince the function approaches the line $y=4$ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we conclude that the equation of the horizontal asymptote is $y=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262142 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "038fb60a-e596-5e48-b05e-f8414d454518" :knowledge-point/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Even Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3399745f-3dc7-5da1-b595-56681144fe67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274063} {:answer/id #uuid "14067442-4764-5e20-ac02-d79d50a4f915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274064} {:answer/id #uuid "2d30e2fa-7524-5892-b158-dc920e01df6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274065} {:answer/id #uuid "f99e1aa0-c431-51b0-9e9b-42e6440e4064" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274066} {:answer/id #uuid "9d7243ed-1cfd-53be-b267-3e5546d33708" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14067442-4764-5e20-ac02-d79d50a4f915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274064} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbae7468-d1b5-5945-ad93-7e8aa2835251" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275097}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dfb95979-045e-5458-8e60-1f524273f5c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-99341" :question/problem "Compute ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\,\\frac{3}{7(x-1)^{4}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{3}{7(x-1)^{4}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(\\frac{3}{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x-1)^{4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{4}}$ as follows:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}.$\n\n- Translate it to the right by $1$ unit. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{4}},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/fc826419a3212b7ce5028a59c90ae43c826734719249119ac3be08386bae4481.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{4}}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThe positive factor of $\\frac{3}{7}$ does not change the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{4}}=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d107785c-82d2-5dbf-9293-e66fad67ba54" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating One-Sided Infinite Limits of Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7d9db7c-9ca1-5a59-b421-c447bb64efa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274073} {:answer/id #uuid "c8ab0069-1429-556d-9021-0afba5e73728" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274074} {:answer/id #uuid "539859c3-ad97-5162-81a7-d5ea56689278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274075} {:answer/id #uuid "4664faf1-45e6-50a8-b9b1-a32c3ddcd7e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274076} {:answer/id #uuid "8c62f80f-81f4-521e-acb6-8231508be3ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c62f80f-81f4-521e-acb6-8231508be3ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274077} :answer-field/id #uuid "40ad97af-e8e5-5ad8-a86d-542e3c4a3431" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275099}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee0af1cf-1b2a-549f-8ef4-b2f7d590c8f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-99658" :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}{log}_{7}(x-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{7}(x-3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/9a25c28ba2bb1c47a32b56620bfb8038d0ec76a51494ee40818c3c18fff94101.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le 3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{log}_{7}(x-3)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>3.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}{log}_{7}(x-3)=-\\infty .\n$$"}]}
